ng state in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of about six points toward the GOP. The race is open, with a term-limited president leaving no incumbent on the ballot, and no candidate fundraising or outside spending figures are available. No general-election polls or expert ratings have been published for the presidential contest, so the forecast rests on the state's fundamentals.\n\nRecent coverage points to an unusually competitive Alaska political environment: prediction markets treat the Senate race as a coin flip, and Mary Peltola's polling strength there suggests Democrats can run close in statewide contests. That justifies a small nudge toward Democrats from the state's baseline lean. Still, presidential results in Alaska have historically run more Republican than its downballot races, so the state stays in the Republican-leaning column with wide uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2436, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3013, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2918, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3121, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2994, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.311, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2901, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.2902, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.2863, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.2839, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.2961, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.2887, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem": 0.3221, "margin": -5.0}]}